; A lunok font: plain text, one glyph per block, `#` is ink and anything else is paper.
;
; The grammar is the one docs/30 says:
;
;   lunok-font 1        the first thing in the file, once
;   width <1..8>        how many columns a glyph has
;   height <1..8>       how many rows a glyph has, and how many lines are read after each `char`
;   gap <0..4>          blank columns between two glyphs
;   char <code>         the code the next `height` lines draw, in order and with no holes
;
; A code this face does not carry is drawn as a gap, so a face with ten digits is a legal face and the text
; around them still lines up. This one is eight by eight and carries the ten digits, which is what `run
; fonts` draws to show that a loaded face and the font of the system can live in the same page.
lunok-font 1
width 8
height 8
gap 1
char 48
.####...
#....#..
#....#..
#....#..
#....#..
#....#..
.####...
........
char 49
...#....
..##....
...#....
...#....
...#....
...#....
.####...
........
char 50
.####...
#....#..
.....#..
....#...
...#....
..#.....
######..
........
char 51
.####...
#....#..
.....#..
..###...
.....#..
#....#..
.####...
........
char 52
....#...
...#....
..#.....
.#..#...
######..
....#...
....#...
........
char 53
######..
#.......
#####...
.....#..
.....#..
#....#..
.####...
........
char 54
..####..
.#......
